<p>Teruel is often the least-visited provincial capital in mainland Spain, and that has practical implications worth knowing before setting out on the return leg to Zaragoza. Restaurant and shop hours can be less flexible than in a major tourist city, many businesses still close for a substantial midday break, and options for last-minute changes — a different pickup time, an extra stop, an unplanned overnight — are more limited simply because there is less infrastructure to absorb them. None of this makes Teruel difficult to visit, but it does reward travelers who plan the practical details, including transport, a little further in advance than they might in Madrid or Barcelona.</p>
